Hi Michael,

The difference with a test case is that it should return an error if
it doesn't work.  In your case, all that involves is adding another
line:

all.equal(z1, z2)

It's obviously not a big deal (especially in this case), but it helps
me spend more time on bug fixing and (a little) less on repeatedly
recreating it. For aaply, in particular, it's also useful to check
multiple dimensions:

z1 <- colMeans(y)
z2 <- aaply(y, 2, mean)
all.equal(z1, z2)

The bug doesn't occur here, so that suggests modifying the test case
to also have a bigger second dimension - that just helps me ensure
that when fixing that bug I don't just inadvertently fix a special
case of it.

As I said, it's not a big deal, but if use plyr a lot and would like
to help make it more reliable (so future versions don't cause this
sort of problem), being able to write test cases is very very helpful
for me.

Hadley

